.TH GETPWENT 3
.SH NAME
getpwent, getpwuid, getpwnam, setpwent, endpwent \- get password file entry
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B "#include <pwd.h>"
.sp
.B "struct passwd *getpwent();"
.sp
.B "struct passwd *getpwuid(uid) int uid;"
.sp
.B "struct passwd *getpwnam(name) char *name;"
.sp
.B "int setpwent();"
.sp
.B "int endpwent();"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR getpwent ,
.BR getpwuid ,
and
.B getpwnam
each return a pointer to an object
with the following structure containing the broken-out fields of a line in
the password file.
.PP
.RS
.nf
.ta .5i 1.1i
struct passwd {
	char	*pw_name;
	char	*pw_passwd;
	int	pw_uid;
	int	pw_gid;
	int	pw_quota;
	char	*pw_comment;
	char	*pw_gecos;
	char	*pw_dir;
	char	*pw_shell;
};
.fi
.RE
.PP
The fields
.I pw_quota
and
.I pw_comment
are unused; the others have meanings
described in
.BR passwd (4).
.PP
.B getpwent
reads the next line (opening the file if necessary);
.B setpwent
rewinds the file;
.B endpwent
closes it.
.PP
.B getpwuid
and
.B getpwnam
search from the beginning until a matching
.I uid
or
.I name
is found (or until
.B EOF
is encountered).
.SH FILES
/etc/passwd
.SH "SEE ALSO"
getlogin(3), getgrent(3), passwd(4)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Null pointer (0) returned on
.B EOF
or error.
.SH BUGS
All information is contained in a static area so it must be copied if it is
to be saved.
.sp
